  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Pleasant conditions through tomorrow as the\nregion sits under high pressure. Lows tonight dip into the 40s\nand 50s.\n\nLarge high pressure continues to make its way over southern New \nEngland through the rest of today, ending up directly overhead \ntonight. With clear skies and light to calm winds overnight, \nradiational cooling is expected to take control, allowing low \ntemperatures to fall into the 40s and 50s tonight. Areas that \nradiate well should drop into the mid 40s while areas closer to \nthe coasts and don't radiate as well will fall into the mid to \nlow 50s overnight.\n\nHigh pressure remains affixed over the region into tomorrow,\nthen shifts off the east coast heading into the evening hours.\n850 mb temperatures remain around 10-14C in the afternoon hours, \nand highs top out in the upper 70s, with areas closer to the \ncoasts in the low 70s with local seabreezes.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Showers, and possibly a few thunderstorms, with \nbrief torrential rainfall possible late Sat night into the first \nhalf of Sun. Low probability for localized flash flooding. \n\nHigh pressure moves off the coast Saturday night, clearing the\npath for a low pressure to pass through Sunday. A larger high \npressure over central Canada and the Northern Plains should \ndrive a cold front well offshore by Monday morning. This will \nresult in a rather brief period of rainfall across southern New \nEngland. The timing for the most likely period of rainfall has \nslowed slightly. It no appears to be some time from late \nSaturday night into Sunday morning. Rainfall should end from \nwest to east Sunday afternoon. Low risk for thunderstorms as \nwell, but not expecting severe weather.\n\nThe most likely outcome is one half to one inch of rainfall for \nmost. However, there is a 35-50% chance for more than one inch \nof rainfall, especially across portions of eastern CT into RI. \nRegardless, it will be quite humid. Thus, localized downpours \nare possible. Will need to continue to monitor the flooding risk\nthis weekend.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Dry and seasonable Mon-Tue. A brief bout of\nsummer warmth and humidity possible Wed into Thu ahead of a \ncold front.\n\nHigh pressure builds back into the region Mon and Tue,returning\ndry and seasonable weather. This high pressure system moves east \nof the region by the middle of next week. This may allow for a \nbrief return of summerlike warmth/humidity some time Wed into \nThu. There is a fair amount of uncertainty on the timing of\nanother cold front Wed night into Thu. With a high pressure over \nthe North Atlantic, would not be overly surprised if this timing\nwinds up being slower with later forecasts. Until this front \npasses us by, expecting above normal temperatures for Wed into \nThu.",
